Shiitake Mushroom Bolognese Bianco
  --Kay Chun
  
3 tbsp evoo, divided
1/2 c finely chopped onion
1/2 c finely chopped carrot
1/4 c finely chopped celery
Kosher salt and freshly ground black pepper
4 oz shiitake mushroom caps, finely chopped (~11/2 c)
3 garlic cloves, minced
1 tbsp white miso
12 oz ground pork
3 c low-­sodium chicken broth
2 tbsp low-­sodium shoyu
1 dried shiitake mushroom
1-­ oz piece guanciale (optional)
1/2 c whole milk
1 small fresh rosemary sprig
3 fresh basil sprigs
1 lb short pasta, such as rigatoni
2 tbsp unsalted butter
1/2 c freshly grated Parmigiano-Reggiano cheese, plus more for serving

In a large Dutch oven or pot, heat 2 tbsp of the oil over medium-­low heat. Add the onion, carrot, and celery, season with salt and black pepper, and cook, stirring occasionally, until softened, ~5 min. Add the shiitake mushroom caps, season with salt and black pepper, and cook, stirring occasionally, until softened, ~3 min. Add the remaining 1 tbsp oil, the garlic, and the miso and cook, stirring, until the miso is lightly caramelized, ~2 min. Add the pork, season with salt and black pepper, and cook, breaking up the pork, until it’s no longer pink, ~3 min. Add the broth, shoyu, dried shiitake, and guanciale (if using) and bring to a simmer. Cover and cook, stirring occasionally, for 30 min. Stir in the milk, rosemary, and basil, cover, and reduce the heat to low. Cook until the liquid is reduced but still saucy, ~15 min. Discard the herb sprigs. Transfer the dried shiitake mushroom and guanciale to a cutting board, finely chop, and add them back to the sauce. When the sauce is almost done, cook the pasta: Bring a large pot of water to a boil. Season the water with salt and cook the pasta according to the package directions until a few min shy of al dente (it will finish cooking in the sauce). Reserve 1 1/2 c of the pasta water and drain. To the bolognese sauce, add the pasta, 1 c of the reserved pasta water, and the butter and bring to a simmer. Cook, stirring constantly, until most of the liquid is absorbed and the pasta is saucy, ~2 min, adding more of the reserved pasta water if necessary. Turn off the heat and stir in the cheese until well incorporated. Season with salt and black pepper. Divide the pasta in bowls. Top with more cheese and black pepper, garnish with parsley and red pepper flakes, if using, and serve warm.





